course: money
lesson: 19
title: Scams: free V-Bucks, fake giveaways, phishing texts
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: A scam is a trick to steal your money, your account or your private details, and scammers dress it up as a gift, a prize, a warning or a bargain.
explain: "FREE V-BUCKS! Just type your login on this site!" is a classic: the V-Bucks are worth £0, but if you type your username and password, the scammer takes your account with £60 of skins on it; tricking people into giving private details is called phishing.
explain: Warning signs: something free or far too cheap (a £100 pair of trainers for £20), a rush ("Your account will be closed! Click now!"), a small payment to get your card details (a £1.45 "parcel fee"), spelling mistakes and odd addresses like "supp0rt@g4mes-help.biz", and anyone asking for a password or a gift card code.
explain: Real companies and banks never ask for your password, never ask for gift card codes and never tell you to move money to a "safe account"; a link can be fake even when it comes from a friend, because their account may have been hacked.
explain: The safe answer is always the same: do not click, do not type, do not pay; block, report and tell a grown-up, and go to the real website or phone number yourself.
fact: A scam is a trick to steal money, an account or private details.
fact: Phishing means tricking someone into giving private details like a login or card number; scam texts are sometimes called smishing.
fact: Real games never give free currency on strange websites, and nobody needs your password to give you anything.
fact: Scam texts often ask for a small payment so they can get your card details and then take much more.
fact: Fake giveaways promise expensive prizes to strangers and often ask for "postage" money or private details.
fact: A price far below normal for a popular new item is a warning sign of fake goods or goods that never arrive.
fact: Companies never ask for gift card codes; scammers like them because the money cannot be traced or returned.
fact: A message from a friend can be a scam if their account was hacked.
fact: Banks never ask you to move money to a "safe account"; hang up and phone the bank yourself on the number on the card.
fact: "Make lots of money fast from home" offers are almost always scams and often ask you to pay first.
fact: Safer websites have the right spelling, a padlock symbol by the address, and are ones you typed yourself; if in doubt, ask a grown-up.
fact: Spelling mistakes, odd email addresses and rushing you are clues that a message is fake.
exercise: What is a scam? | A trick to steal money, an account or private details | A real prize from a game company | A type of bank account | A special offer in a shop
exercise: A video says "Free V-Bucks, just type your login here." What would really happen? | The scammer could take your account | You would get 10,000 free V-Bucks straight away | The game would send you a thank-you prize | Nothing, because logins can't be used by others
exercise: Sam's account has £60 of skins. If he loses it to a scam, how much is lost? | £60 | £0 | £30 | £120
exercise: What is phishing? | Tricking people into giving private details | Catching fish off the coast to sell for money | Sending a real parcel | Saving money in a bank
exercise: A text says "Your parcel is waiting, pay £1.45." Mum isn't expecting a parcel. What should she do? | Delete it | Pay it, it's only £1.45 | Reply asking which parcel | Send her card number to check
exercise: Why do scam texts ask for a small payment like £1.45? | To get your card details and take much more | Because postage really does cost £1.45 these days | To test whether your phone is working properly | To give you a refund later when the parcel comes
exercise: A comment promises a £500 PS5 to the first 100 people. What would 100 consoles cost? | £50,000 | £5,000 | £500,000 | £600
exercise: Someone in a game chat says "Give me your password first, then I'll give you a rare skin." What should Lucy do? | Say no, block and report them | Give her password, the skin is rare | Give half of her password, just to be safe | Ask him to send two skins first as proof
exercise: Which is a clue that an email is a scam? | Spelling mistakes and an odd address | It comes on a Tuesday, when scams are most common | It is short and polite | It has the game's real logo
exercise: Trainers that cost £100 are sold for £20 on a strange site. How much off is that? | £80 | £20 | £120 | £50
exercise: A man says he's from the game company and wants a £20 gift card code. What is true? | Real companies never ask for gift card codes | He must be real if he knows the game and her username | It's fine if it's under £20 | Gift card codes are safe to share
exercise: Harry's hacked mate's account sends "free skins" links to 50 friends, and 5 click. How many more accounts may be stolen? | 5 | 50 | 45 | 1
exercise: A caller says "This is your bank, move your money to a safe account." What should Ellie's mum do? | Hang up and phone the bank on the number on the card | Move the money quickly to be safe | Give the caller Grandad's card number | Ask the caller to ring back tomorrow when Mum is at home
exercise: Grandad has £2,000 savings. How much would he lose if he moved it to the "safe account"? | £2,000 | £0 | £200 | £1,000
exercise: An ad says "Make £500 a day from home". How much would that be in 30 days? | £15,000 | £1,500 | £5,000 | £150,000
exercise: Which three checks make a website safer? | Right spelling, a padlock, and an address you typed yourself | Bright colours, big buttons and a timer | Lots of ads, pop-ups and prizes | A cartoon logo, music and free gifts
test: Sam's brother: "That's a scam, ya muppet, they'll nick your account." ("Muppet" means silly person; "nick" means steal.) What should Sam do with the free V-Bucks site? | Close it and never type his login | Type a fake password to trick them | Try it once to see | Share it with friends first
test: Rio's like "free PS5, first 100 to DM, no cap!" Dad goes "nah". What would most likely happen if Rio messaged? | He'd be asked for details or "postage" money and get nothing | A PS5 would arrive next week | He'd win and so would his mates | Nothing at all, it's harmless fun
test: Kai's cousin: "£20 trainers? Mate, that's well dodgy." ("Dodgy" means suspicious.) What's most likely? | Fakes, or nothing ever arrives | A real bargain the shop forgot to fix | A gift from the trainer company | A normal price for new trainers
test: A text from Harry's mate's account: "OMG free skins here!!" Harry tells the group "don't click it." Why, if it's from a mate? | His mate's account was hacked, so the link is fake | Mates never send links | Free skins only come by post | The link is real but just slow because so many people click it
test: Zoe gets: "I'm from the game. Send a £20 gift card code to fix your account." A Kiwi mate says "yeah nah, that's a scam, bro." ("Yeah nah" means no.) What does Zoe do? | Tell her mum and send nothing | Send £10 first just to test if it's real | Send the code, he knows the game | Ask him to send proof of who he is first
test: Leila's sis says "total scam, babe" about "£500 a day from home, kids welcome". Why is that so clearly a trick? | No real job pays a child £15,000 a month from home | Because it's only £500 | Because kids aren't allowed on the internet | It's real but only on weekends
test: Email: "URGENT!!! Ur acount will be closed in 1 hour! Click here!" from "supp0rt@g4mes-help.biz". Count the clues it's fake. | Rushing you, spelling mistakes and an odd address | Only the exclamation marks at the end of each line | None, because it looks completely official with a logo | Only the one-hour timer at the top of the message
test: Grandad's phone: "This is your bank, move your £2,000 now!" Grandad says "but he knew my name, love." What's true? | Scammers can know names; banks never ask you to move money | Knowing your name proves it's the bank | Move half to be safe | Banks often call to move money
test: What would happen if Lucy gave her password "just this once" to someone promising a rare skin? | She'd probably lose her account and get no skin | She'd get the skin and still keep her account safe | Her password would change by itself | The game would reward her honesty
test: Charlie's mum shows him a site called "Fortnlte-Free.com" with no padlock that came from a pop-up. How many of the 3 safety checks does it pass? | None | One | Two | All three
test: A Singapore cousin messages "send me your login can or not, I gift you skin lah." ("Can or not" means can you; "lah" adds friendliness.) Best reply? | No: nobody needs your login to give you anything | Yes, cousins can always have your login | Send half of it to be safe | Send it, then change it next year
test: Mum isn't expecting a parcel but the text says "£1.45 or it goes back!" Her mate says "just pay, it's tiny." What's the real danger? | Giving the scammers her card details so they take far more | Losing exactly £1.45 and nothing else | The parcel will be sent to the wrong house | The post office will be cross
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises "phishing"? | The fraudulent inducement of a person to disclose private details | The lawful sale of freshly caught fish by electronic means to the public | The dispatch of a parcel by a recognised courier | The deposit of money in a savings account
exam: Identify the course of action most consistent with the established guidance where a caller, purporting to represent a bank, requests the transfer of savings to a "safe account". | Termination of the call and contact with the bank by its published number | Immediate transfer of the savings as instructed | Transfer of one half of the savings as a precaution | Disclosure of the card number for verification
exam: It is given that one hundred consoles are each valued at five hundred pounds. Compute the aggregate value purportedly offered by the giveaway. | Fifty thousand pounds | Five thousand pounds | Five hundred thousand pounds | Six hundred pounds
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning requests for gift card codes by persons claiming to represent a company. | Genuine companies never make such requests, the funds being untraceable | Such requests are customary and may be safely granted | Such requests are genuine where the sum is below twenty pounds | Such requests are genuine where the caller knows the account holder's name and address
